- our car Chester on tonight Zimmerman  says he was told he'd be testing a  sporty car with a rear engine he wasn't  expecting this though he says that  Renault believes even a pocket rocket  deserves a rear engine so he's going to  check out the benefits  the ancient sound certainly isn't going  to set a sports color lovers heart  racing with its 52 kilowatts that  twinkle crawls rather than Sprint's from  0 to 100 kilometres an hour taking 14  and a half seconds but thanks to its  optional start/stop system the Twingo  already meets EU emission norms of 95  grams of co2 per kilometer which only  take effect in the year 2020  before Tyler Andre says the benefits of  the rear engine and rear-wheel drive are  obvious as they don't have to transfer  power the front wheels can focus solely  on steering reducing the danger of under  steering and the engineers have created  another advantage in doing this it's big  steering angle makes the Twingo easy to  maneuver in city traffic the Twingo can  do a u-turn on the narrowest of streets  giving you the ability to change  directions and will of course you'll  need to keep an eye out for oncoming  traffic  Andre says the high seating position in  the Twingo doesn't make for a sporty  ride but it does produce good visibility  between those rearview mirrors right at  his eye level so he can see right away  what's behind him though in multi lane  traffic gets confusing as the mirror  hangs right in front of his face  visiting inside the Twingo is colorful  and imaginative though it contains lots  of hard plastic there's even a removable  storage bin in front of the gearshift  unfortunately the gear stick isn't very  ergonomic by contrast the climate  control system is quite user friendly  the same can't be said for the radio  Andre says his test model has no  integrated sat-nav it does have a cell  phone holder but it makes it hard to see  and use the buttons behind it put the  phone in landscape mode to use a  navigation app for instance and it  covers all the buttons using another  external sat-nav rather than a phone app  could also be problematic normally you'd  want to attach it here on the front  windshield but the cable would have to  go in the first 12 volt socket here the  Twingo is a colorful little city  runabout for the urban jungle round  daytime running lights are integrated  into the bumpers the trim at the side of  the car sports the model name and the  concept drawing of the Twingo though the  looks of the real car doesn't quite  measure up for 990 euros extra the  Twingo comes as a convertible with a  folding soft top  the one liter engine is located in the  back under the trunk vents let the hot  air escape Andre says the Twingo has 219  liters of volume more than the Opel Adam  but a little less than VW's up the  Twingo could use a bit more space as the  trunk won't close when these three  crates are put in lengthwise he says  making the trunk lid a bit thinner here  could likely solve the problem I'll  admit that's probably my shyness unglued  shift the crates to the centre of the  trunk though and the lid closes easily  as the plastic reinforcements are much  thinner there so what's Andres verdict  on the new Twingo  DeNooyer he feels it's a really great  city card it stops in terms of  maneuverability and visibility at faster  speeds he was pleasantly surprised that  unlike front-wheel drive cars  it was never unstable while braking  Andre wonders whether Renault will bring  out an RS version as he thinks this  Twingo has sporting potential
 

